<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jbala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jbala is a mountainous region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a> and the Rif, and the name of the Arabic-speaking people who live there. Its hills, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chefchaouen">Chefchaouen</a>, were long largely independent of the sultans and were a centre of resistance to the Portuguese and later the Spanish. From 1912 to 1956 most of it formed part of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in Morocco.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jbala.jpg" alt="Jbala" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar-Toons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jbalas_w._L%C3%A9gende.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settled among the <b>Jbala</b> tribes of the Rif, they partly rebuilt their power, acknowledging now Córdoba, now the Fatimids: al-Qasim al-Gannun ruled for the Fatimids from 938 to 948; his son Abul-'Aysh turned to the Umayyads and left for al-Andalus in 954; Jawhar's invasion of 958 restored Fatimid…</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirs ruled the western Rif and <b>Jbala</b> hills semi-independently, nominally recognising the Wattasid Sultanate in Fez.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-chefchaouen">Emirate of Chefchaouen</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Fez created the French protectorate in Morocco in 1912, a Franco-Spanish treaty of November 1912 assigned Spain a northern zone along the Mediterranean coast, including the Rif and <b>Jbala</b> mountains, and a southern zone around Cape Juby.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-protectorate-in-morocco">Spanish protectorate in Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
